# keur quality control — source file over ~300 net lines.
#
# Why this exists: keur's own norm (AGENTS.md) is "files well under ~300 net
# lines; split by responsibility." That was enforced by scripts/check-basics.sh,
# an external shell lint. This rule captures the file-size half of that check as
# a real, in-engine control — fix + control, and one less thing that lives
# outside the binary. The directory-count half of check-basics stays in C++/shell
# because it is a cross-file aggregation (files per directory), which a per-file
# rule engine cannot express.
#
# HOW: the `max-lines` engine counts a file's NET lines. With skip_comments the
# scanner strips comment lines to blanks first, so blank + comment lines do not
# count — matching check-basics.sh's `grep -vcE '^\s*(//|/*|*|$)'`. Fires once
# per over-long file, at line 1.
#
# WARN, not block: file length is a maintainability smell, not a bug. It nudges
# toward splitting by responsibility. Tests are excluded (a big test file is
# often legitimately one cohesive suite); generated/vendor/build dirs too.
#
# @see docs/rules-authoring.md (`max-lines`), AGENTS.md ("Files small"),
#      scripts/check-basics.sh (the shell lint this replaces).
id: KEUR-QUAL-005
title: Source file over ~300 net lines (split by responsibility)
category: quality
severity: warning
engine: max-lines
action: warn
skip_comments: true
min_count: 300
target:
  extensions: .c .cpp .cc .h .hpp
  exclude_paths: test/ tests/ _test.cpp vendor/ build/ dist/
patterns:
  - regex: file too long
    message: "Source file exceeds ~300 net lines — hard to hold in one head. Split it by responsibility (one clear job per file). See AGENTS.md 'Files small' and docs/rules-authoring.md (max-lines). @see KEUR-QUAL-005"
confidence: 0.6
fix: "Split the file along its responsibilities into smaller siblings (e.g. one .h/.cpp per logical unit), the way learn.cpp was split into learn/history + learn/sysinfo + learn/monitor. Keep a cohesive test suite as one file if splitting would fragment it."
